tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: Aqueducts", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "Two-tier arcade into a castellum with three lead mains.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Rome, Aqua Appia 312 BCE to Aqua Alexandrina 226 CE", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Gravity-fed channels that brought spring water tens of kilometres to cities, mostly", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "underground, with bridges and arcades only where valleys demanded. Rome's eleven lines", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "delivered on the order of a million cubic metres a day.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Surveyors (using the chorobates water level and the groma) set gradients as shallow as 1", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "in 3000; the Pont du Gard carries its channel 50 km on a fall of 17 metres. Where a valley", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "was too deep for arches, an inverted siphon of lead pipes carried water down and up again", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "under pressure. In the city a distribution tank (castellum) split flow to baths, fountains", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "and private houses.", "color": "gray"}
